1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
HAZAL: Tom, could I ask you for some advice, please?

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,340
Tom: Yes of course, if you think I can help. What's it about?

3
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:14,697
HAZAL: It's my first media studies assignment, and I'm not sure how to go about it.

4
00:00:14,697 --> 00:00:17,040
You must have done it last year.

5
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:22,820
Tom: Is that the one comparing the coverage of a particular story in a range of newspapers?

6
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:23,730
HAZAL: That's right.

7
00:00:23,730 --> 00:00:26,940
Tom: Oh yes, I really enjoyed writing it.

8
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:30,130
HAZAL: So what sort of things do I need to compare?

9
00:00:30,130 --> 00:00:32,407
Tom: Well, there are several things.

10
00:00:32,407 --> 00:00:35,340
For example, there's the question of

11
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:38,100
which page of the newspaper the item appears on.

12
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:43,291
Hazel: You mean, because there's a big difference between having it on the front page

13
00:00:43,291 --> 00:00:46,060
and the bottom of page ten, for instance?

14
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:51,550
Tom: Exactly. And that shows how important the editor thinks the story is.

15
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:57,500
Then there's the size - how many column inches the story is given

16
00:00:58,180 --> 00:01:00,390
how many columns it spreads over.

17
00:01:00,390 --> 00:01:03,730
Hazel: And I suppose that includes the headline.

18
00:01:03,730 --> 00:01:07,840
Tom: It certainly does. It's all part of attracting the reader's attention.

19
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:13,430
Hazel: What about graphics - whether there's anything visual in addition to the text?

20
00:01:13,430 --> 00:01:15,817
Tom: Yes, you need to consider those,

21
00:01:15,817 --> 00:01:20,932
too, because they can have a big effect on the reader's understanding of the story -

22
00:01:20,932 --> 00:01:23,660
sometimes a bigger effect than the text itself.

23
00:01:23,660 --> 00:01:27,720
Then you'll need to look at how the item is put together

24
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:30,090
what structure is it given?

25
00:01:30,090 --> 00:01:35,147
Bear in mind that not many people read beyond the first paragraph,

26
00:01:35,147 --> 00:01:38,940
so what has the journalist put at the beginning?

27
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:42,789
And if, say, there are conflicting opinions about something,

28
00:01:42,789 --> 00:01:47,920
does one appear near the end, where people probably won't read it?

29
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:53,730
Hazel: And newspapers sometimes give wrong or misleading information, don't they?

30
00:01:53,730 --> 00:01:58,890
Either deliberately or by accident. Should I be looking at that, too?

31
00:01:58,890 --> 00:02:00,823
Tom: Yes, if you can.

32
00:02:00,823 --> 00:02:05,580
Compare what's in different versions, and as far as possible

33
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:08,170
try and work out what's true and what isn't.

34
00:02:08,170 --> 00:02:11,993
And that relates to a very important point:

35
00:02:11,993 --> 00:02:19,160
what's the writer's purpose, or at least the most important one, if they have several.

36
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:22,961
It may seem to be to inform the public,

37
00:02:22,961 --> 00:02:26,380
but often it's that they want to create fear

38
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:30,140
or controversy, or to make somebody look ridiculous.

39
00:02:30,140 --> 00:02:32,800
Hazel: Gosh, I see what you mean

40
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:36,950
And I suppose the writer may make assumptions about the reader.

41
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:41,790
Tom: That's right - about their knowledge of the subject, their attitudes,

42
00:02:41,790 --> 00:02:43,580
and their level of education

43
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:48,830
which means writing so that the readers understand without feeling patronised.

44
00:02:48,830 --> 00:02:53,070
All of that will make a difference to how the story is presented.

45
00:02:53,070 --> 00:02:59,680
Hazel: Does it matter what type of story I write about?

46
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:03,381
Tom: No - national or international politics, the arts...

47
00:03:03,381 --> 00:03:08,470
Anything, as long as it's covered in two or three newspapers.

48
00:03:08,470 --> 00:03:12,500
Though of course it'll be easier and more fun

49
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:15,110
if it's something you're interested in and know something about.

50
00:03:15,110 --> 00:03:20,075
Hazel: And on that basis a national news item would be worth analysing -

51
00:03:20,075 --> 00:03:25,040
I'm quite keen on politics, so I'll try and find a suitable topic.

52
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:28,550
What did you choose for your analysis, Tom?

53
00:03:28,550 --> 00:03:33,352
Tom: I was interested in how newspapers express their opinions explicitly,

54
00:03:33,352 --> 00:03:37,282
so I wanted to compare editorials in different papers,

55
00:03:37,282 --> 00:03:41,600
but when I started looking

56
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:45,140
I couldn't find two on the same topic that I felt like analysing.

57
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:47,990
Hazel: In that case, I won't even bother to look.

58
00:03:47,990 --> 00:03:52,131
Tom: So in the end I chose a human interest story -

59
00:03:52,131 --> 00:03:56,647
a terribly emotional story about a young girl who was very ill,

60
00:03:56,647 --> 00:03:59,760
and lots of other people - mostly strangers

61
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:02,670
raised money so she could go abroad for treatment.

62
00:04:02,670 --> 00:04:09,113
Actually, I was surprised - some papers just wrote about how wonderful everyone was,

63
00:04:09,113 --> 00:04:15,060
but others considered the broader picture, like why treatment wasn't available here.

64
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:20,770
Hazel: Hmm, I usually find stories like that raise quite strong feelings in me!

65
00:04:20,770 --> 00:04:22,410
I'll avoid that.

66
00:04:22,410 --> 00:04:24,880
Perhaps I'll choose an arts topic,

67
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:28,640
like different reviews of a film

68
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:31,880
or something about funding for the arts - I'll think about that.

69
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:34,040
Tom: Yes, that might be interesting.

70
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:38,220
Hazel: OK, well thanks a lot for your help, Tom. It's been really useful.

71
00:04:38,220 --> 00:04:41,290
Tom: You're welcome. Good luck with the assignment, Hazel.
